DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine CDC Confirms Case of Measles in Maine
DESCRIPTION:Sep 16\, 2026\nThe Maine Department of Health and Human Servic
 es’ Center for Disease Control and Prevention (Maine CDC) is reporting a
  case of measles in Maine\; the first case in the state since five cases w
 ere confirmed in February of this year. The Maine CDC confirmed the latest
  case on September 15\, 2026. According to\nthe U.S. Centers for Disease C
 ontrol and Prevention (U.S. CDC)\,\nas of September 11\, there were 3\,294
  confirmed measles cases reported nationwide in 2026.\nThe individual is a
 n unvaccinated adult from Somerset County. The Maine CDC is working with t
 he facility where potential exposure occurred and is working with them to 
 inform potentially exposed individuals.\nIndividuals who were at the follo
 wing location* during the timeframe listed below were potentially exposed 
 to measles and should take precautions and should watch for symptoms for 2
 1 days after September 12:\nLocation\nDate\nTime\nMaineGeneral Express Car
 e\, Waterville 211 Main Street\, Waterville\, ME\nSeptember 12\, 2026\n8am
 -11am\n*Subject to change as the investigation continues\nMeasles is a hig
 hly contagious viral disease. If one person has measles\, 90% of the peopl
 e close to that person who are not immune will become infected. Symptoms o
 f measles include:\nFever\nCough\nRunny nose\nRed\, watery eyes\nRash that
  spreads from the head down.\nIndividuals who develop symptoms should cont
 act their health care provider for instructions\nbefore\ngoing to the prov
 ider’s office or hospital. This will help prevent the spread of further 
 infection. If symptoms are consistent with measles\, a health care profess
 ional may test to determine a measles infection.\nMeasles can cause severe
  sickness including pneumonia\, encephalitis (brain swelling)\, and death.
  An infected person spreads measles through coughs or sneezes. Once infect
 ed\, a person is contagious from four days before their rash starts throug
 h four days afterwards. The virus remains alive for up to two hours on sur
 faces and in the air. The period from exposure to onset \nhttps://www.main
 e.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-confirms-case-measles-maine-2026-09-16
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-confirms-case-measles-maine-
 2026-09-16
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
